speechtext:
I do not know what the hon. gentleman means. As has been said more than once, the people of Canada are watching the results of the movements in this House, and of every member who is responsible for precipitating another general election they will ask:	Why did you do it;
what was the question involved? How much 14011-322
more rapidly could any set of men go in the direction of reforming the Customs service than we did? What more honourable man can you find in the public service than the present Minister of Customs? I am ready for the contest at any time, but I do not want the country to be precipitated into another general election without due deliberation, I do not think it is wise to make a leap in the dark. Therefore I would exhort every hon. gentleman who intends to come back here to weigh well the results of his vote to-night.
